# x86-64 only, on top of common.list (MAD-846, ADR 0013). The kernel and
# live-boot come from the live-build config (x86/live), not from here.

# What Raspberry Pi OS Lite brings and a bare Debian root doesn't: the
# logind session the kiosk's PAMName=login needs (seat access for cage),
# the D-Bus it talks over, a text font for Chromium, and a busybox for the
# initramfs's mount (the overlay script's lowerdir= options).
libpam-systemd
dbus
fonts-dejavu-core
busybox

# Firmware for common PC hardware (non-free-firmware): GPUs, Wi-Fi, the
# audio DSPs of recent Intel machines (SOF, with the UCM profiles
# PipeWire needs to see their outputs), and CPU microcode.
firmware-linux-free
firmware-misc-nonfree
firmware-intel-graphics
firmware-amd-graphics
firmware-iwlwifi
firmware-realtek
firmware-atheros
firmware-sof-signed
alsa-ucm-conf
intel-microcode
amd64-microcode

# The installer (syncphony-install, the "Install to disk" boot entry):
# partitioning, the filesystems, and GRUB for both firmware types. The
# -bin packages only: grub-pc and grub-efi-amd64 would try to reinstall
# the bootloader on every update.
fdisk
dosfstools
e2fsprogs
grub2-common
grub-pc-bin
grub-efi-amd64-bin
whiptail

# Maintenance: an installed box can get an admin user from the installer
# (there is no Imager here), and SSH to reach it.
sudo
openssh-server
